WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.400
Discrete autoregressive models paint an image one token at a time.

2
00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:06.700
At high resolution, that sequence gets long, and slow.

3
00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:10.900
Compress the image harder, and the sequence gets short,

4
00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:14.600
but each token now has to carry far more detail.

5
00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:18.700
Meet GSTAR: group-scaled token capacity.

6
00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:22.700
Each position stores a group of G discrete codes.

7
00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:26.500
Double the compression, quadruple the group: the bit budget stays the same.

8
00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:30.700
Packing 2×2 turns a 32× tokenizer into an effective 64× one, losslessly.

9
00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:34.700
Now every step has to emit many correlated codes.

10
00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:38.500
A causal head is slow. A parallel head is fast, but its codes can collide.

11
00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:42.700
Our semi-autoregressive head: one parallel pass, then a one-layer causal sweep.

12
00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:46.900
Add CUDA Graphs, fused kernels and SGLang serving,

13
00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:50.700
and a 1024 × 1024 image takes 2.5 seconds on one H200.

14
00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:55.300
That is up to 21× faster than prior discrete autoregressive models,

15
00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:59.700
and GSTAR-Pro-8B leads every open model we compare on Qwen-Image-Bench.

16
00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:05.600
It edits images too, scoring 7.45 on GEdit-Bench.

17
00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:12.400
GSTAR. Short sequences, rich tokens, high-quality images.
